{
  "result": "There is a particular kind of silence that arrives just before rain. The world does not become quiet exactly; rather, its noises seem to move farther away. Cars soften on the road, leaves turn their pale undersides to the wind, and even birdsong feels like a memory being folded shut.\n\nI like that moment because it asks nothing of us. We do not need to be productive beneath a darkening sky. We can stand by a window and watch the first drops gather themselves, hesitate, and fall. Rain makes ordinary things briefly theatrical: a gutter becomes a river, a porch light becomes a small moon, and a person hurrying home becomes the main character in a story no one else will read.\n\nPerhaps this is why weather affects us so deeply. It gives shape to feelings that are otherwise difficult to name. A bright morning can lend courage. Fog can make uncertainty look almost beautiful. Snow can persuade a crowded city to slow down and remember its own outline.\n\nWhen the storm passes, the world is never quite the same. Pavement shines. Air tastes newly washed. Puddles hold broken pieces of the sky. Nothing important has been solved, yet everything seems possible for a few minutes.\n\nI think hope often works this way. It does not arrive with trumpets or explanations. It gathers quietly at the edge of things, waits through the gray, and then begins\u2014not by changing the world completely, but by making the familiar world visible again.",
